                                            "Plural des Substantivs Pronom"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "form-of",
                                            "plural"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d3a7e789-f7c7-473d-a8f6-22e5125e7ee1",
                        "source_entry_id": "a2b418ac-a19f-384d-90f0-ca6dccfcfb65",
                        "source_sense_id": "afc5a695-bbdc-3a3f-8160-7112ba881759",
                        "source_sense_description": "een woord dat verwijst naar iets anders zonder ze te noemen",
                        "target_lang_word": "pronoun",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "pronoun",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "12b03518-c985-3fcd-a42d-39fd95ca6b31",
                                        "entry_id": "8ad3969a-1dbf-3c4b-8a43-40da045aa373",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A type of word that refers anaphorically to a noun or noun phrase, but which cannot ordinarily be preceded by a determiner and rarely takes an attributive adjective."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5434a1fb-1679-4cb2-95ec-1cfb6bbba51e",
                                                "sense_id": "12b03518-c985-3fcd-a42d-39fd95ca6b31",
                                                "text": "The possessive conjunctive pronoun is always repeated before a substantive, and after a conjunction; as my brothers and sisters, mes frères & mes sœurs; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "826a0423-ef56-42b9-99de-0175d272b22e",
                                                "sense_id": "12b03518-c985-3fcd-a42d-39fd95ca6b31",
                                                "text": "Dalia: Why are you playing the pronoun game?\nAlyssa: What? What are you talking about? I'm not even.\nDalia: You are. \"I met someone.\" \"We have a great time. \"They're from my home town.\" Doesn't this tube of wonderful have a name!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3a5a5595-9cde-4d55-b148-dba07657efb9",
                                                "sense_id": "12b03518-c985-3fcd-a42d-39fd95ca6b31",
                                                "text": "As here the possessive pronoun 'our' has inclusive reference in that it a priori includes both the editor and reader, its presence amounts to a kind of pronominal bonding between writer and reader."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "bbe4e420-1394-3238-81b7-0c353c978ceb",
                                        "entry_id": "8ad3969a-1dbf-3c4b-8a43-40da045aa373",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Ellipsis of preferred pronoun, any of the third-person pronouns by which a person prefers to be referred to, typically reflecting their gender identity; often communicated as a subject–object pair."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "abbreviation",
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "ellipsis",
                                            "in-plural",
                                            "specifically"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "76e94c24-0d57-468e-bb2e-4c752f67362a",
                                                "sense_id": "bbe4e420-1394-3238-81b7-0c353c978ceb",
                                                "text": "My pronouns are she/her. What are your pronouns?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5f94d35b-3685-4863-881d-87b289eec73b",
                                                "sense_id": "bbe4e420-1394-3238-81b7-0c353c978ceb",
                                                "text": "The vast majority (82 percent) of the nonbinary trans students I interviewed used nonbinary pronouns for themselves, and all said that they were rarely given the opportunity to indicate their pronouns."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "15ff10dd-eb4c-4e57-a014-d60935b1cf73",
                                                "sense_id": "bbe4e420-1394-3238-81b7-0c353c978ceb",
                                                "text": "Then an earnest elaboration: “It’s just nice that other people understand what I’m thinking. I don’t have to explain a million things. I don’t have to be like, Okay, I guess I’ll let you ignore my pronouns. It’s a very good space.”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "59836e78-fd39-49bd-831a-8f8e35ff5fd5",
                                                "sense_id": "bbe4e420-1394-3238-81b7-0c353c978ceb",
                                                "text": "You can refuse to use pronouns and alternate names and still show love to your child. Be clear and succinct."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2c39927e-a1cc-4ed2-b6f7-dd7c835885b9",
                                                "sense_id": "bbe4e420-1394-3238-81b7-0c353c978ceb",
                                                "text": "Abbott celebrated the move on X, sharing a report from the Austin American-Statesman on the firing and writing: “A Texas state employee refused to remove pronouns from email signature. He was fired before noon.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "94b6f6cb-72ff-4ec3-a6eb-244503464df3",
                        "source_entry_id": "a2b418ac-a19f-384d-90f0-ca6dccfcfb65",
                        "source_sense_id": "afc5a695-bbdc-3a3f-8160-7112ba881759",
